        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"alcott-little-women-8286ffd0e0a0d0d3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Amy’s embedded “I love you” is principally a sincere avowal of her present loving state. It also provides marital reassurance, but the words themselves only weakly invoke commitment; the undertaking is conveyed more by her surrounding claims that she would marry and share hardship with Laurie. Her emotional delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is slight T/P ambiguity because the avowal occurs within reassurance about Amy’s willingness to remain with Laurie in poverty, but the core love clause reports feeling more directly than it undertakes an obligation.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Amy directly addresses Laurie; the core love avowal is grammatically embedded in “that I might show how much I love you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Laurie jokingly recalls Amy’s former ambition to marry wealth, and Amy answers by denying that money motivated her choice. Her love avowal supports the claim that she would have married him without money and functions as sincere reassurance within their marriage. The narrator’s reference to “convincing proofs of the truth of her words” strongly validates the avowal and implies private affection. The exclamatory protest and affectionate aftermath indicate intensity, but not a reflex-like production of the words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I sometimes wish you were poor that I might show how much I love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The clause explicitly presents Amy’s loving emotional state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I’d have married you if you hadn’t a penny” and Amy’s later claim that she would “gladly pull in the same boat” with Laurie.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding speech frames the avowal as reassurance about steadfast partnership under poverty, giving limited indirect support to P.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Amy ... gave convincing proofs of the truth of her words.”\",\"supports\":\"The narrator explicitly endorses the sincerity and truth of Amy’s avowal rather than presenting it as deception or empty convention.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amy begins with the emotional protest “Oh, my dearest boy, don’t, don’t say that!” but places the love clause within an articulated explanation of why money did not determine her marriage.\",\"supports\":\"The intensity belongs to a deliberate avowal and reassurance; there is no positive evidence that “I love you” is a reflex-like expressive discharge, supporting E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is in Chapter Forty-Four of Louisa May Alcott’s Little Women and is tagged as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its present affirmative linguistic form.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the central avowal, while a low P score reflects its secondary role in marital reassurance. Emotional intensity and affectionate conduct are contextual features adequately represented without E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
